From: squadai-stable <noreply@local>
Date: 2026-06-19
Subject: [PATCH] R4 gate (0031) — confine every active lever to the eGPU

Plan B2 of CLAUDE.md / docs/egpu-driver-dkms-reference.md. The prior
build (aorus12) broke the INTERNAL dGPU (RTX PRO 4000, 10de:2c39 @
01:00.0) with Xid 79 because NO lever had a device gate: the recovery
machinery fires on any NVIDIA GPU the driver binds. During a normal
RTD3/GC6 resume the internal GPU's BAR firewall briefly reads
0xFFFFFFFF, the levers read that as "GPU lost", and reset/disconnect
the internal — exactly the R4 violation.

This patch adds a single source-of-truth predicate
os_pci_is_egpu_target() (topology: behind a Thunderbolt/USB4 controller;
plus device-id 0x2d04 as a fallback) and gates EVERY active lever on it,
so recovery is confined to the external eGPU and the internal dGPU is
never touched:

  - os-pci.c / os-interface.h (x2): define + declare the predicate.
  - nv-pci.c error_detected: non-eGPU -> stock M-base DISCONNECT
    (never NEED_RESET -> never pci_reset_bus).
  - nv-pci.c slot_reset/resume: defensive guards.
  - nv-pci.c probe: only spawn the Q-watchdog kthread for the eGPU.
  - nv-lever-m-recover.c trigger_post_rminit_fail: early-return for
    non-eGPU (this is the pci_reset_bus-on-upstream-bridge path).
  - nv-lever-m-recover.c G3-H: only rewrite AER UncMask on the eGPU.
  - os.c Q-active: only declare disconnected / set_disconnected for the eGPU.

Satisfies R3 (eGPU still recovers / drops alone) and R4 (internal dGPU
never reset or marked disconnected) together.
---
diff --git a/kernel-open/common/inc/os-interface.h b/kernel-open/common/inc/os-interface.h
index 0728057..33bc9de 100644
--- a/kernel-open/common/inc/os-interface.h
+++ b/kernel-open/common/inc/os-interface.h
@@ -116,6 +116,12 @@ void        NV_API_CALL  os_pci_remove                    (void *);
 //
 NvBool      NV_API_CALL  os_pci_is_disconnected           (void *);
 void        NV_API_CALL  os_pci_set_disconnected          (void *);
+//
+// AORUS R4 gate (Patch 0031): is this device the external Thunderbolt eGPU
+// (legit target of the recovery levers) vs the internal dGPU (never touch)?
+// Single source of truth gating every active lever. See os-pci.c.
+//
+NvBool      NV_API_CALL  os_pci_is_egpu_target            (void *);
 void*       NV_API_CALL  os_map_kernel_space              (NvU64, NvU64, NvU32);
 void        NV_API_CALL  os_unmap_kernel_space            (void *, NvU64);
 #if defined(NV_VMWARE)
diff --git a/kernel-open/nvidia/nv-lever-m-recover.c b/kernel-open/nvidia/nv-lever-m-recover.c
index c082ead..7ba4e29 100644
--- a/kernel-open/nvidia/nv-lever-m-recover.c
+++ b/kernel-open/nvidia/nv-lever-m-recover.c
@@ -45,6 +45,7 @@
 #include "nv-linux.h"
 #include "nv-pci-types.h"
 #include "nv-qwatchdog.h"  /* tb_egpu_qwatchdog_aer_snapshot */
+#include "os-interface.h"  /* AORUS R4 gate: os_pci_is_egpu_target (Patch 0031) */
 
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/workqueue.h>
@@ -1035,6 +1036,12 @@ int tb_egpu_lever_m_trigger_post_rminit_fail(nv_linux_state_t *nvl)
     if (!nvl)
         return 0;
 
+    /* AORUS R4 gate (Patch 0031): the post-rmInit-FAIL path schedules a
+     * pci_reset_bus on the upstream bridge. On the internal dGPU that would
+     * reset its CPU root port — the Xid-79 path. Only ever fire for the eGPU. */
+    if (!os_pci_is_egpu_target(nvl->pci_dev))
+        return 0;
+
     if (!NVreg_TbEgpuLeverMRecoverEnable || !nvl->lever_m)
         return 0;   /* M-base fallback: leave existing path in place */
 
@@ -1351,7 +1358,10 @@ int tb_egpu_lever_m_recover_init(nv_linux_state_t *nvl)
      * Runs regardless of NVreg_TbEgpuLeverMRecoverEnable since unmasking
      * is independent of the recovery state machine — even with M-recover
      * disabled, we want errors visible rather than silently demoted. */
-    if (NVreg_TbEgpuUncMaskClearEnable && nvl->pci_dev)
+    /* AORUS R4 gate (Patch 0031): only rewrite AER UncMask on the eGPU;
+     * never touch the internal dGPU's error-reporting config. */
+    if (NVreg_TbEgpuUncMaskClearEnable && nvl->pci_dev &&
+        os_pci_is_egpu_target(nvl->pci_dev))
     {
         struct pci_dev *pdev = nvl->pci_dev;
         int aer_pos = pci_find_ext_capability(pdev, PCI_EXT_CAP_ID_ERR);
diff --git a/kernel-open/nvidia/nv-pci.c b/kernel-open/nvidia/nv-pci.c
index f403749..279d04b 100644
--- a/kernel-open/nvidia/nv-pci.c
+++ b/kernel-open/nvidia/nv-pci.c
@@ -2224,7 +2224,12 @@ nv_pci_probe
      * so the device stays in D0 and the kthread can safely read MMIO
      * indefinitely. Failure here is non-fatal — driver continues without
      * watchdog, falling back to the existing Q-active wrapper. */
-    (void)tb_egpu_qwatchdog_init(nvl);
+    /* AORUS R4 gate (Patch 0031): only run the heartbeat kthread for the
+     * external eGPU. On the internal dGPU the kthread's PMC_BOOT_0 reads
+     * would see a transient 0xFFFFFFFF during a normal RTD3/GC6 resume and
+     * call os_pci_set_disconnected on it — exactly the Xid-79 path. */
+    if (os_pci_is_egpu_target(pci_dev))
+        (void)tb_egpu_qwatchdog_init(nvl);
 
     /* AORUS Lever M-recover init was moved earlier in probe (line ~1920)
      * in Commit 2 so the WPR2 check has lever_m available; no longer
@@ -2876,6 +2881,17 @@ nv_pci_error_detected(struct pci_dev *pci_dev, pci_channel_state_t state)
     if (nvl)
         lm = nvl->lever_m;
 
+    /* AORUS R4 gate (Patch 0031): never drive a bus reset on anything but
+     * the external Thunderbolt eGPU. For the internal dGPU (or any non-eGPU)
+     * fall back to stock M-base DISCONNECT — exactly the upstream behaviour,
+     * so the kernel's normal teardown runs and the internal is never reset. */
+    if (!os_pci_is_egpu_target(pci_dev))
+    {
+        result = PCI_ERS_RESULT_DISCONNECT;
+        reason = "M-base (R4 gate: not the eGPU target)";
+        goto log_and_return;
+    }
+
     if (!NVreg_TbEgpuLeverMRecoverEnable || !lm)
     {
         /* M-base behaviour (Enable=0 OR per-device state not allocated). */
@@ -2945,11 +2961,18 @@ log_and_return:
  * in nv-lever-m-recover.c so the recovery logic stays in one TU. */
 static pci_ers_result_t nv_pci_slot_reset(struct pci_dev *pci_dev)
 {
+    /* AORUS R4 gate (Patch 0031): defensive — error_detected never returns
+     * NEED_RESET for a non-eGPU, so the kernel should not call slot_reset
+     * here, but never run the recovery verify/reset path on the internal. */
+    if (!os_pci_is_egpu_target(pci_dev))
+        return PCI_ERS_RESULT_DISCONNECT;
     return tb_egpu_lever_m_slot_reset(pci_dev);
 }
 
 static void nv_pci_resume(struct pci_dev *pci_dev)
 {
+    if (!os_pci_is_egpu_target(pci_dev))
+        return;
     tb_egpu_lever_m_slot_reset_resume(pci_dev);
 }
 
diff --git a/kernel-open/nvidia/os-pci.c b/kernel-open/nvidia/os-pci.c
index 46f459a..ae449fb 100644
--- a/kernel-open/nvidia/os-pci.c
+++ b/kernel-open/nvidia/os-pci.c
@@ -188,6 +188,39 @@ void NV_API_CALL os_pci_set_disconnected(
     WRITE_ONCE(pdev->error_state, pci_channel_io_perm_failure);
 }
 
+//
+// AORUS R4 gate (Patch 0031, 2026-06-19): is this device the external
+// Thunderbolt eGPU (the recovery levers' legitimate target), as opposed
+// to the internal dGPU which must NEVER be touched?
+//
+// Single source of truth for every active lever (M-recover bus reset,
+// Q-watchdog kthread, Q-active set_disconnected, G3-H AER UncMask clear).
+// On this dual-GPU laptop the internal RTX PRO 4000 (10de:2c39 @ 01:00.0)
+// hangs off a CPU root port and its transient 0xFFFFFFFF reads during a
+// normal RTD3/GC6 resume were mis-read as "GPU lost", so the ungated
+// levers reset/disconnected it (Xid 79). Gating every active path on this
+// predicate keeps recovery confined to the eGPU.
+//
+// Primary gate: topology — the device sits behind a Thunderbolt/USB4
+// controller (pci_is_thunderbolt_attached walks the bridge chain). The
+// internal dGPU is not TB-attached, so it can never match.
+// Secondary gate: device-id 0x2d04 (AORUS RTX 5060 Ti), belt-and-suspenders
+// in case TB attribution is ever missing for the tunnelled GPU.
+//
+NvBool NV_API_CALL os_pci_is_egpu_target(
+    void *handle
+)
+{
+    struct pci_dev *pdev = (struct pci_dev *) handle;
+    if (!pdev)
+        return NV_FALSE;
+    if (pci_is_thunderbolt_attached(pdev))
+        return NV_TRUE;
+    if (pdev->vendor == 0x10de && pdev->device == 0x2d04)
+        return NV_TRUE;
+    return NV_FALSE;
+}
+
 NV_STATUS NV_API_CALL
 os_enable_pci_req_atomics(
     void *handle,
